shocking news just kidding not shocking  at all a new study finds that raising  the minimum wage a sort of reasonable  amount relative to the cost of living  has economic benefits and generally  improves people's lives many  right-wingers will tell you that if you  raise the minimum wage right especially  to $15 an hour as many Democrats want to  do it'll be the apocalypse businesses  will close widespread unemployment  unfortunately the facts don't really  bear that out we have a new study  conducted by UC Berkeley which proves  the opposite they looked at Seattle  Washington and they looked at Seattle's  $15 minimum wage and it worked really  really well the study used the rest of  Washington's economy as sort of control  to predict what would have happened in  Seattle if they had not increased the  minimum wage to $15 On January 1st of  this year the $15 minimum wage took  effect and now unemployment is lower  than it's been in Seattle for nearly a  decade in Seattle wages are up  unemployment is down and the number of  hours worked is up meaning that  employers aren't cutting hours to  account for the higher minimum wage this  was even true in the restaurant industry  even at fast-food restaurants which is a  common example from the right the  Conservatives like to say oh well it's  when you raise the minimum wage it's  particularly bad for fast-food  restaurants and the truth is people  weren't being laid off and the way  Republicans said would happen the higher  minimum wage drew people to those  fast-food jobs not away and those  workers are also able to themselves as a  result of earning a higher salary have a  higher standard of living and these  these are meaningful trends right the  data here is really impactful because  Seattle didn't just suddenly raise its  minimum wage six months ago they've been  slowly and steadily raising it over the  last several years up to $15 per hour at  the beginning of this year and things  have been progressively getting better  and better since the beginning of these  increases the study found that instead  of the new minimum wage making it harder  for businesses to find what are called  low skilled employees it's causing  surrounding communities to raise their  wages to compete with the new  opportunities in Seattle companies are  struggling  when they're not paying their workers  enough instead of the other way around  you hero with minimum wage goes up that  will make companies struggle no the  company's not paying the higher wages  are struggling and some of this is  pretty intuitive right if people have  more money to spend because the minimum  wage went up that will help businesses  including fast food since people earning  in that range are more likely to go to  fast food restaurants as well and more  and more people are working they're  working more hours they're getting paid  more  all of which stimulates the economy  Seattle is the first major city to raise  its minimum wage in this way and this is  now going to become part of a larger  study at Berkeley to look at other  cities raising their minimum wages this  is very good news right and  conservatives have this talking point  that if you raise the minimum wage then  you have to make up for it by lowering  hours or cutting staff in some way as if  there's no other factors there and I  understand that you know a company may  budget out a certain amount to labor  costs or but you there's other things in  that pie chart right like for example  corporate profits and when you're  talking about big companies like  McDonald's or Walmart they can easily  afford to do that no doubt about it and  it is not it is not a linear  relationship right if you raise wages X  you don't have to raise all prices that  exact same percentage and by the way  there's another really common  conservative talking point from those  who try to debunk the minimum wage which  is listen if $15 is better than $12 and  more money for everybody is good why not  make the minimum wage $200 an hour and  then everything would be great see you  couldn't so you shouldn't raise the  minimum wage at all no one is asking for  that that is a total red herring that is  not an equilibrium wage given the cost  of living and productivity and what you  need to make in order to have a livable  wage there's no economic argument for a  $200 an hour minimum wage just because  $15 is better than 12 or maybe 18 would  be better than 15 it doesn't mean that  any serious people are calling for a  $200 minimum wage so please do not fall  for that when right-wingers say that if  raising the minimum wage is good just  keep on raising it it's not a serious  economic argument that anybody is making
 

